#lead[Hallowell County should hold its construction awards under the current rules. Every township has a block marked served, so no township is eligible. Even granting full-block coverage to all terrestrial claims, three townships miss a 25% initial access floor.]

The 120-block file contains 14,496 households. In North Ridge, the only township explicitly identified as northern, the provider map flags blocks containing 1,908 of 2,135 households. Only 377 households are in blocks with a terrestrial claim. Satellite-coded blocks account for the difference of 1,531 households. Depending on address-level evidence, between 0 and 1,531 could be reclassified within those blocks. A satellite-only correction leaves four flagged blocks in North Ridge and therefore still releases no construction funds under the township rule.

We formulate a binary deployment model for fiber and fixed wireless access. It accounts for distance to backbone, terrain, installation and ten years of discounted maintenance. Under an alternative that permits investment in provider-unserved blocks and uses a common #value("budget") cap, the benchmark adds #value("t2_main_hh") qualifying household opportunities at a lifecycle cost of #value("t2_main_cost"). #value("sensitivity_summary")

#value("recommendation_summary") #value("override_summary") #value("equity_summary")

An address audit is the first step. Construction should follow only when the funding authority approves the revised eligibility and providers validate the sites. Southern fixed wireless projects should be assessed using address-level orderability, busy-hour performance, total price, device access and successful work or school use.

